<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <title><%= content_for(:title) || "Checkers" %></title>
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1">
    <meta name="apple-mobile-web-app-capable" content="yes">
    <meta name="application-name" content="Checkers">
    <meta name="mobile-web-app-capable" content="yes">
    <%= csrf_meta_tags %>
    <%= csp_meta_tag %>

    <%# Turbo 8 fetches a link's page when the pointer rests on it, to make the click feel
        instant. Opening an invite link is what takes the free seat in a match, so a fetch
        nobody asked for is a join nobody asked for: prefetching is off for the whole
        application. Nothing here is slow enough to need it. %>
    <meta name="turbo-prefetch" content="false">

    <%= yield :head %>

    <%# The three icons below are the Rails generated favicons under public/. They are the
        only image files in the project and nothing in the game uses an image. %>
    <link rel="icon" href="/icon.png" type="image/png">
    <link rel="icon" href="/icon.svg" type="image/svg+xml">
    <link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="/icon.png">

    <%# Includes all stylesheet files in app/assets/stylesheets %>
    <%= stylesheet_link_tag :app, "data-turbo-track": "reload" %>
    <%= javascript_importmap_tags %>
  </head>

  <body>
    <a class="skip-link" href="#main">Skip to the main content</a>

    <header class="masthead">
      <div class="masthead__inner">
        <%= link_to "Checkers", root_path, class: "masthead__brand" %>

        <nav class="masthead__nav" aria-label="Main">
          <%# My games is there for everyone, signed in or not: a guest's matches are listed
              under the key in their browser's cookie, and become the account's the moment
              they sign up or sign in. %>
          <%= link_to "My games", matches_path, class: "masthead__link" %>

          <% if authenticated? %>
            <span class="masthead__identity"><%= Current.user&.display_name %></span>
            <%# A full page load, like signing in and signing up: it ends the document, and the
                Action Cable socket with it, at the moment this browser stops being this user
                (see the note on the sign-in form). %>
            <%= button_to "Sign out", session_path, method: :delete,
                  class: "button button--quiet", form: { data: { turbo: false } } %>
          <% else %>
            <%= link_to "Sign in", new_session_path, class: "masthead__link" %>
            <%= link_to "Sign up", new_registration_path, class: "masthead__link" %>
          <% end %>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </header>

    <main id="main" class="page">
      <%= render "layouts/flash_zone" %>
      <%= yield %>
    </main>

    <footer class="colophon">
      <p>English draughts. The rules live in a pure-Ruby engine; the pages are Rails.</p>
    </footer>

    <%# The one live region on the page, and the reason it is out here.

        Every accepted action answers with a Turbo Stream that replaces the whole status
        fragment, and that fragment used to hold the only role="status" paragraph on the page.
        Replacing a live region detaches it and inserts a new one, and a region that did not
        exist before the change is a region a screen reader has nothing to compare against, so
        the opponent and every viewer were told nothing when a move arrived (round-2 audit,
        finding M2; measured statusStillInDocument false, anyAriaLive 0). This element is
        outside every id a stream replaces, so it survives the update, and the announcer
        controller on the status fragment writes the new status sentence into it. It is empty in
        the server's HTML on purpose: a region is announced for what is put into it after the
        page settles, not for what it was born with, and with JavaScript off there is no update
        to announce and the status paragraph is simply read where it stands. %>
    <div id="live-announcer" class="visually-hidden" role="status" aria-live="polite"
         aria-atomic="true"></div>
  </body>
</html>
